0">1 · Meet Our Star</option><option value="1">2 · A Giant Energy Factory</option><option value="2">3 · The Sun Is Never Still</option><option value="3">4 · Exploring Our Nearest Star</option></select></div><div class="control-field"><label for="voice">Reading voice</label><select id="voice"><option value="">Device default</option></select></div><div class="playback"><button id="read" type="button">▶ Read section</button><button id="pause" type="button" disabled>Ⅱ Pause</button><button id="stop" type="button" disabled>■ Stop</button></div><a class="pdf-link" href="sun-reader.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↓ Open print PDF</a><span class="current-section" id="current-section">Section 1: Meet Our Star</span><span id="status" aria-live="polite">Choose a section to hear it.</span></div><aside class="overview" id="overview" hidden aria-label="Home-language section summary"><strong>Section summary · Home-language bridge</strong><p id="summary-text"></p><small>English stays visible. Use Translate below each reading paragraph for help. Read section plays the summary when a home language is selected.</small><div class="review-note">Translations are a learning aid and need teacher verification.</div></aside></details><main id="reader"><section class="page" id="p1" aria-labelledby="title-0"><div class="hero"><img src="assets/images/sun.jpg" alt="" fetchpriority="high"><div class="hero-title"><div class="eyebrow">YOUR NEXT MISSION / OUR NEAREST STAR</div><h1 id="cover-title">THE SUN</h1><p>The Star That Powers Our World</p><span class="issue-tag">SCIENCE READER / 04</span></div></div><p class="cover-credit">The Sun in extreme ultraviolet light, September 10, 2025. Colors are assigned to show details invisible to our eyes. Credit: NASA/GSFC/SDO. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/image-of-sun-from-nasas-solar-dynamics-observatory/">NASA image record</a></p><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">01 / MEET OUR STAR</div><h2 id="title-0">Meet Our Star</h2><p class="dek">What if the Sun disappeared?</p><p class="readpara" data-p="0-0">Imagine waking up one morning, but the Sun never rises. The sky stays dark. Days pass, and Earth becomes colder and colder. Plants stop receiving the sunlight they need to make food. Life on our planet would change dramatically. Fortunately, the Sun is still shining!</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-0-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="0-1">The Sun is a star—a gigantic, glowing ball of extremely hot material called plasma. It is made mostly of hydrogen and helium. It sits at the center of our solar system. Its powerful gravity keeps Earth and the other planets traveling around it.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-0-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="0-2">The Sun is enormous. About 1.3 million Earths could fit inside it by volume!</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-0-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-2" hidden></p><figure class="diagram-panel" tabindex="0" aria-label="Teaching diagram"><picture><source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="assets/size-mobile.svg"><img src="assets/size.svg" alt="The Sun and Earth diameters are shown to scale, with an enlarged view of Earth. About 1.3 million Earths fit inside the Sun by volume."></picture><figcaption class="diagram-caption">Diameters at left are to scale; Earth is also shown enlarged. Separation is not to scale. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/">NASA Sun facts</a> · <a href="assets/size.svg">Open larger diagram</a></figcaption></figure><div class="facts" aria-label="Sun fact file"><div class="fact"><b>150 million <span>km</span></b><small>Average distance from Earth</small></div><div class="fact"><b>4.5 billion</b><small>Years old, approximately</small></div><div class="fact"><b>8 min 20 sec</b><small>Approximate travel time of sunlight to Earth</small></div></div><div class="callout "><b>WOW FACT</b><p>The sunlight reaching Earth right now began its journey about eight minutes ago. We see the Sun as it was a little earlier, not exactly as it is at this moment!</p></div><div class="callout "><b>THINK ABOUT IT</b><p>Why is the Sun important to Earth even though it is so far away?</p></div></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · THE SUN</span><b>01 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p2" aria-labelledby="title-1"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">02 / ENERGY IN MOTION</div><h2 id="title-1">A Giant Energy Factory</h2><p class="dek">Where does sunlight come from?</p><figure id="inside-sun" class="diagram-panel interior-panel" aria-label="Inside the Sun diagram"><p class="diagram-scroll-note">Swipe or scroll sideways to see the full diagram.</p><div class="diagram-window" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Sun interior diagram, scroll sideways on small screens"><img src="assets/interior.svg?v=2" alt="A simplified cross-section labels the core, radiative zone, convection zone, and visible surface; energy moves outward."></div><figcaption class="diagram-caption">Original teaching diagram. Colors and layer widths are schematic. The energy journey from core to surface takes a very long time. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/">NASA Sun facts</a> · <a href="assets/interior.svg?v=2">Open larger diagram</a></figcaption></figure><p class="readpara" data-p="1-0">Deep inside the Sun, something extraordinary is happening. The Sun&#x27;s center, called its core, is incredibly hot—about 15 million degrees Celsius!</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-1-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="1-1">Here, tiny hydrogen nuclei join together to form helium. This process is called nuclear fusion. It releases enormous amounts of energy. That energy eventually reaches the Sun&#x27;s outer layers and travels into space as light and other forms of radiation.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-1-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="1-2">Some of that energy reaches Earth. Sunlight warms the land and oceans. Plants use light energy to make food. Solar panels can turn sunlight into electricity. Without the Sun&#x27;s energy, Earth would be a very different world.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-1-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-2" hidden></p><figure class="diagram-panel" tabindex="0" aria-label="Teaching diagram"><picture><source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="assets/energy-mobile.svg"><img src="assets/energy.svg" alt="Fusion releases energy in the core; energy moves outward over a long time; sunlight travels to Earth in about eight minutes; plants use light to make food."></picture><figcaption class="diagram-caption">An energy pathway; distances and sizes are not to scale. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/">NASA Sun facts</a> · <a href="assets/energy.svg">Open larger diagram</a></figcaption></figure><div class="callout "><b>BIG QUESTION</b><p>How can energy made inside the Sun help a plant grow on Earth?</p></div></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · THE SUN</span><b>02 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p3" aria-labelledby="title-2"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">03 / SPACE WEATHER</div><h2 id="title-2">The Sun Is Never Still</h2><p class="dek">Can a star have storms?</p><p class="readpara" data-p="2-0">From Earth, the Sun may look like a calm, bright circle in the sky. But scientists know it is full of activity. Sometimes, dark patches appear in images of the Sun. These are called sunspots. They are areas that are cooler than the bright surface around them, although they are still extremely hot.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-2-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="2-1">The Sun also has powerful magnetic fields. Sometimes, they release energy in enormous bursts called solar flares. The Sun continuously sends tiny charged particles into space. This moving stream is called the solar wind. When these particles interact with Earth&#x27;s magnetic field and atmosphere, they can help create beautiful curtains of light in the sky. These glowing lights are called auroras.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-2-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-1" hidden></p><div class="visual-grid"><figure class=""><img src="assets/images/sunspots.jpg" alt="A group of dark sunspots on the Sun’s bright disk." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Sunspot group NOAA 12192, observed by SDO/HMI in October 2014. Credit: NASA. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/sunspots/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><figure class=""><img src="assets/images/flare.jpg" alt="A long filament erupts from the Sun." loading="lazy"><figcaption>A solar eruption associated with a flare, August 31, 2012. SDO/AIA; extreme ultraviolet shown in assigned color. Credit: NASA/SDO. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/magnificent-outburst/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><figure class=""><img src="assets/images/aurora.jpg" alt="Green and purple aurora above the curved Earth at night." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Aurora above Earth, September 7, 2026; photographed from the ISS. Credit: NASA / ISS Crew Earth Observations Facility. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/purple-haze-aurora/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure></div><p class="readpara" data-p="2-2">Powerful activity on the Sun can also affect satellites, radio communication, and electrical systems. Scientists study the Sun so they can better understand and predict this activity, called space weather.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-2-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-2" hidden></p><div class="callout "><b>SCIENTIST&#x27;S NOTE</b><p>Not all Sun images show colors that human eyes would see. Scientists sometimes use special colors to represent different kinds of light, making otherwise invisible details easier to study.</p></div><div class="callout "><b>THINK ABOUT IT</b><p>How can activity on the Sun affect people living on Earth?</p></div></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · THE SUN</span><b>03 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p4" aria-labelledby="title-3"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">04 / EXPLORING OUR NEAREST STAR</div><h2 id="title-3">Exploring Our Nearest Star</h2><p class="dek">How can scientists study something so hot?</p><figure class="wide-photo"><img src="assets/images/parker.png" alt="An artist’s illustration of Parker Solar Probe near the Sun." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Parker Solar Probe, artist’s illustration. Credit: NASA/APL. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/parker-solar-probe/nasas-parker-solar-probe-makes-history-with-closest-pass-to-sun/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><p class="readpara" data-p="3-0">No human could stand on the Sun. It is far too hot, and unlike Earth, it does not have a solid surface to walk on. So how do scientists learn about it? They use special telescopes, powerful instruments, and robotic spacecraft.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-3-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="3-1">NASA&#x27;s Solar Dynamics Observatory studies activity across the Sun. It records images using different wavelengths of light, helping scientists observe changes that our eyes cannot see. Another spacecraft, Parker Solar Probe, travels incredibly close to the Sun. It gathers information about the Sun&#x27;s outer atmosphere and the particles flowing into space.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-3-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="3-2">Scientists use this evidence to investigate how the Sun works and how its activity affects the solar system. There are still mysteries to solve. For example, the Sun&#x27;s outer atmosphere, called the corona, is much hotter than the visible layer below it. Scientists are investigating why. The next time you feel warm sunlight on your face, remember that you are feeling energy from a star about 150 million kilometers away.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-3-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-2" hidden></p><div class="callout "><b>A SUN-SAFETY RULE</b><p>Never look directly at the Sun without proper solar-viewing protection. Ordinary sunglasses are not safe for viewing the Sun. Scientists use instruments designed to observe it safely.</p></div><div class="callout "><b>MISSION QUESTION</b><p>If you could send a scientific spacecraft to study the Sun, what would you want it to discover?</p></div><h3>Five mission words</h3><p class="support-note">Look, say, explain: use each illustration to help a partner understand the word.</p><div class="word-grid"><div class="word"><img src="assets/word-star.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true"><b>star</b><span>A huge, glowing ball of hot material that produces its own energy.</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/word-gravity.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true"><b>gravity</b><span>A force that attracts objects toward one another.</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/word-fusion.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true"><b>nuclear fusion</b><span>A process in which small atomic nuclei join and release energy.</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/word-flare.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true"><b>solar flare</b><span>A sudden, powerful burst of energy from the Sun.</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/word-evidence.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true"><b>evidence</b><span>Information or observations that help support an explanation.</span></div></div><h3>Think like a scientist</h3><div class="questions"><p class="question"><b>01</b> Why is the Sun a star rather than a planet?</p><p class="question"><b>02</b> How does the Sun help living things on Earth?</p><p class="question"><b>03</b> What happens during nuclear fusion inside the Sun?</p><p class="question"><b>04</b> How can solar activity affect Earth?</p><p class="question"><b>05</b> Why do scientists use spacecraft and special instruments to study the Sun?</p></div><h3>Speak &amp; write</h3><div class="frames"><p class="frame">The Sun is a star because ____.</p><p class="frame">The Sun&#x27;s gravity ____.</p><p class="frame">Energy from the Sun helps ____ because ____.</p><p class="frame">Scientists observed ____, which is evidence that ____.</p><p class="frame">I would investigate ____ because ____.</p></div><div class="sources"><b>Explore the evidence</b><p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/">NASA: Sun facts</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/sunspots/">NASA: sunspots</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/parker-solar-probe/nasas-parker-solar-probe-makes-history-with-closest-pass-to-sun/">NASA: Parker Solar Probe</a> · <a href="credits.html">Image and science credits</a></p></div></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · THE SUN</span><b>04 / 04</b></div></section></main><footer class="site-footer"><p>Issue 04 · Prepared for instructional review · English article with home-language bridges</p><p><a href="credits.html">Sources &amp; image credits</a> · <a href="sun-reader.pdf">Print edition</a></p></footer><script src="app.js?v=2" defer></script></body></html>